[AWARD_DESC.RUS_1]
MEDAL FOR COMBAT MERITS
\n The Medal for Combat Merits was instituted on October 17, 1938, together with the Medal of Valor. This medal was awarded for efficient and courageous action in battle, for valor demonstrated while defending the borders of the USSR, for excellence in combat training and political indoctrination, new equipment practice, and for maintaining operational readiness during active service.
\n The medal was awarded primarily to soldiers and sergeants, and less frequently to non-commissioned officers. During the Great Patriotic War, it was awarded quite frequently, and it was not unusual for a single person to be awarded with it twice. Occasionally civilians were awarded with this medal as well.

[AWARD_DESC.RUS_2]
MEDAL OF VALOR
\n The Medal of Valor was instituted on October 17, 1938, and thus it was the second Soviet medal, after the service medal for the 20th anniversary of the Red Army. It is worth noting that there is a certain piece of military hardware depicted upon its face – a T-35 five-turret heavy tank.
\n The medal was awarded primarily to soldiers and sergeants - and less frequently to non-commissioned officers - for unique demonstrations of personal courage. Thus these medals were highly appreciated in the field. Over 4,230 thousand such awards were granted during WWII. It was not unheard of a single person to be awarded with this medal more than once; there were holders of four or even five such medals. Foreign residents were sometimes awarded with Medal for Valor as well; these were mainly civilians who assisted Soviet troops.

[AWARD_DESC.RUS_3]
ORDER OF THE RED STAR
\n The Order of the Red Star was instituted on April 6, 1930, and was the second Soviet military order ever instituted. It was awarded for exceptional service in the defense of the USSR in both war and peace, and for enhancing national security.
\n Since the beginning of the war, the Order of the Red Star became the most widespread military order. 2,860 thousand awards were granted from 1941 to 1945 for combat valor. It was not unheard of a single person to receive this award more than once; there were holders of four or even five such orders. It was also awarded to many units, subunits and enterprises, as well as to several citizens of foreign countries.

[AWARD_DESC.RUS_4]
PARTISAN OF THE PATRIOTIC WAR, 2ND CLASS
\n This order was instituted on May 20, 1942, and was the first order to be instituted during the war. It was also the first USSR order to have classes. It was awarded to soldiers of the rank and file, to the commanding officers of the Red Army, Navy, and NKVD forces, and to partisan parties who demonstrated courage, spirit, and bravery in battles for the Soviet Motherland. It was also awarded to service members who conducted successful field operations.
\n A total of 951,652 of these awards granted during the war. Some veterans had three or four orders of both classes. It was the only Soviet order that was given to the family of the awardee in the case that the award was granted posthumously. It also stayed with the families of departed decoration holders.

[AWARD_DESC.RUS_5]
ORDER OF ALEXANDR NEVSKY
\n This order was instituted on July 29, 1942, and was the only Soviet award to have a pre-revolutionary name. It was awarded to commanding officers of the Red Army who displayed personal courage, bravery and spirit in battles for Motherland, and who assured the success of their units by their skillful command.
\n The Order of Alexandr Nevsky was a rather rare award, and was highly prized by veterans. A total of 42,165 such awards were granted during the war, which included 1,473 awards to formations of the Red Army, and about 70 awards to officers of the Allied forces. It is of interest that despite the relative rarity of the order, there were quite a number of cases where the same person received the award twice, and two cases where the award was granted thrice.

[AWARD_DESC.RUS_6]
ORDER OF THE RED BANNER
\n The Soviet government of Russia instituted the Order of the Red Banner on September 16, 1918, and the all-Soviet Order of the Red Banner was instituted on August 1, 1924. It was the first Soviet order, and the highest one (except for the Order of Lenin, which was not just a military order, and the very rare Order of Victory meant for the highest military command). It was awarded for extraordinary courage, dedication and bravery demonstrated in the defense of the socialist Motherland.
\n During the years of the World War II, 238 thousand of these awards were granted, most part of which took place from 1943 to 1945. It was rather uncommon for soldiers, sergeants and non-commissioned officers to be decorated with this order (except for airmen). Most of the order's holders were commissioned, field, and general officers.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_1]
DAS KRIEGSVERDIENSTKREUZ 2.KLASSE
\n The War Merit Cross was founded on October 18, 1939.
\n The War Merit Cross Second Class was awarded to military personnel for valor in battle and for meritorious service behind the lines. It became the most popular award of the Third Reich, with the number of awardees numbering some 6 million. As a result, by the end of the war its prestige was only moderate.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_2]
DAS KRIEGSVERDIENSTKREUZ 1.KLASSE
\n The War Merit Cross was founded on October 18, 1939.
\n The War Merit Cross First Class was awarded to military personnel who had been previously awarded the War Merit Cross Second Class, for valor in battle and for meritorious service behind the lines.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_3]
DAS EISERNE KREUZ 2.KLASSE
\n The Iron Cross was founded on March 10, 1813, and re-instituted on September 1, 1939. The Cross was awarded for bravery and heroism displayed on the battlefield.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_4]
DAS EISERNE KREUZ 1.KLASSE
\n The Iron Cross 1st Class. Originally founded in 1813, it re-appeared during the Franco-Prussian War and World War I, and was restored once again on September 1, 1939. The Cross was awarded for the accomplishment of three to five especially difficult missions, and for valor shown on the battlefield.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_5]
KRIEGSORDEN DES DEUTSCHEN KREUZES
\n The German Cross award was instituted on September 28, 1941. It was awarded to members of the Wehrmacht, and Party personnel, as well as to auxiliary services such as police, military railway service, and so on. It was presented for successful accomplishment of six to twelve especially difficult missions, if the decorated person had already been awarded the Iron Cross 1st Class.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_6]
DAS RITTERKREUZ DES EISERNEN KREUZES
\n The Knight's Cross of the Iron Cross was the highest class of the Iron Cross military order. It was introduced on September 1, 1939 upon the reinstitution of the Iron Cross. It was worn on a ribbon around the neck. It had several degrees. The most widespread degree was the Iron Cross itself.
\n The Cross was awarded to the Wehrmacht, the SS, and Party personnel for exceptional military merit.

[AWARD_DESC.GER_7]
DAS RITTERKREUZ DES EISERNEN KREUZES MIT EICHENLAUB
\n This award was established on July 15, 1941 specifically for the forces fighting in Russia, and later in other countries, to increase the morale of the soldiers and officers.
\n The Cross was awarded for accomplishment of especially difficult missions, and for valor on the battlefield.

[AWARD_DESC.UK_1]
BRITISH EMPIRE MEDAL
\n In 1917, to fill a gap in the British award system, King George V instituted the 5-class British Empire Medal, to be awarded for both military and civilian service.
\n Since 1940, the Medal has been awarded to military personnel for meritorious service in battle. Often it was presented to persons favorably mentioned in reports, for notable feats which nevertheless fell short of a higher-class award.

[AWARD_DESC.UK_2]
MILITARY MEDAL
\n The Military Medal was instituted on March 16, 1916 as an equivalent to the Military Cross, and was intended for presentation to privates and non-commissioned officers. It often required a mention in a written order. The Medal was awarded to military personnel of the British army, and armies of the Commonwealth countries, for bravery in battle.

[AWARD_DESC.UK_3]
MEMBER OF THE ORDER OF THE BRITISH EMPIRE
\n In 1917, to fill a gap in the British award system, King George V founded the Order of the British Empire, an order of chivalry awarded for military and civilian service worthy of recognition by the crown. Military servicemen received higher or lower classes depending on their rank: higher classes where usually presented to generals, while lower classes were awarded to officers, sergeants and privates.

[AWARD_DESC.UK_4]
DISTINGUISHED SERVICE ORDER
\n This order was established in 1886 to reward officers for meritorious or distinguished service in war, but whose actions fell short of the requirements for receiving the Victoria Cross.

[AWARD_DESC.USA_1]
LEGION OF MERIT - LEGIONNAIRE
\n The Legion of Merit was instituted on July 20, 1942 in four degrees: Chief Commander, Commander, Officer, and Legionnaire. The first three may only be awarded to members of foreign armed forces, and the fourth is reserved for U.S. military personnel. The medal is awarded for exemplary conduct, bravery, and heroism in the performance of outstanding services.

[AWARD_DESC.USA_2]
SILVER STAR
\n The Silver Star was instituted on July 9, 1918. It was originally known as the Citation Star, but on July 16, 1932, by the order of the Secretary of War, it was renamed the Silver Star. It is awarded for exceptional valor in action.

[AWARD_DESC.USA_3]
DISTINGUISHED SERVICE MEDAL
\n This medal was instituted on January 2, 1918. It is bestowed on any servicemen whose wartime actions were acknowledged as highly fruitful, or actions which were significantly helpful in extraordinary peacetime situations. Civilians can also receive this decoration, when they display extraordinary merits which satisfy the requirements of the award.

[AWARD_DESC.USA_4]
MEDAL OF HONOR
\n This medal was instituted under order of President Lincoln’s on July 12, 1862. Initially it was given only to the lowest ranks of enlisted personnel, for exceptional deeds on the battlefield. Later, an Act of Congress dated March 3, 1863 made it available to officers. \n
The award is bestowed for valor, bravery, and self-sacrifice shown under great personal risk. To qualify for this award, the recipient's deed must have been witnessed by many as something unique and extraordinary.

[AWARD_DESC.ITA_1]
CROCE AL VALORE MILITARE
\n The Cross for Military Valor was established in 1941. The award was a slightly modified Croce al Merito di Guerra (War Merit Cross).
\n It was awarded to members of the Italian armed forces for exemplary conduct or battlefield bravery.

[AWARD_DESC.ITA_2]
MEDAGLIA DI BRONZO AL VALOR MILITARE
\n The Bronze Medal for Military Valor was established in 1883. It was awarded to members of the Italian armed forces for battlefield bravery.

[AWARD_DESC.ITA_3]
MEDAGLIA D'ARGENTO AL VALOR MILITARE
\n The Silver Medal for Military Valor was established in 1883. It was awarded to members of the Italian armed forces for battlefield heroism.

[AWARD_DESC.ITA_4]
MEDAGLIA D'ORO AL VALOR MILITARE
\n The Gold Medal for Military Valor was established in 1883. It was awarded to members of the Italian armed forces for extreme acts of heroism. The medal is one of the highest awards in Italy, and has been awarded only very rarely.

[AWARD_DESC.PRK_1]
ORDER OF FREEDOM AND INDEPENDENCE
\n This order is awarded to division and brigade commanders (1st class), and lower-ranked officers (2nd class), for military service.

[AWARD_DESC.PRK_2]

MILITARY MERIT MEDAL
\n The Military Merit Medal was established on July 13, 1949, before the onset of the Korean War. It was awarded for 10 years of faultless service. Chinese volunteers received it as well.

[AWARD_DESC.PRK_3]

ORDER OF THE NATIONAL FLAG, 2ND CLASS
\n The Order of the National Flag was established on October 2, 1948, and was awarded in one of three classes. It was granted for exceptional military or civil service in the political, cultural, or economical sphere.

[AWARD_DESC.PRK_4]

ORDER OF THE NATIONAL FLAG, 3RD CLASS
\n The Order of the National Flag was established on October 2, 1948, and was awarded in one of three classes. It was granted for exceptional military or civil service in the political, cultural, or economical sphere.

[AWARD_DESC.FRA_1]
MEDAILLE MILITAIRE
The medal was instituted by the decree of January 22, 1852 and later on preserved by the Republic. It is issued to any enlisted personnel and civilians for military merit.
[AWARD_DESC.FRA_2]
CROIX DE GUERRE 1939-45
The Croix de Guerre was introduced on September 28, 1939. It was similar to the WWI Croix de Guerre.
[AWARD_DESC.FRA_3]
CROIX DU COMBATTANT
The Croix Du Combattant was introduced on June 28, 1930 to distinguish those who had been issued the so-called combatant’s card. Later on, the Croix Du Combattant became a distinguishing decoration for enlisted personnel who had participated in military actions.

[AWARD_DESC.POL_1]
ORDER VIRTUTI MILITARI V KLASA
It was established on August 1, 1919 by the Polish Parliament. The Virtuti Militari Order became its official title since 1933. It was bestowed on servicemen and civilians regardless of their position or background: for wise war leadership, for initiative on the battlefield which resulted in a great victory over the enemy; and for exceptional valor and courage on the battlefield.
[AWARD_DESC.POL_2]
ORDER VIRTUTI MILITARI IV KLASA
It was established on August 1, 1919 by the Polish Parliament. The Virtuti Militari Order became its official title since 1933. It was bestowed on servicemen and civilians regardless of their position or background: for wise war leadership, for initiative on the battlefield which resulted in a great victory over the enemy; and for exceptional valor and courage on the battlefield. It was awarded if the person had already been bestowed with the Virtuti Militari Order 5th Class.
[AWARD_DESC.POL_3]
KRZYZ WALECZNYCH
The Cross of the Valorous was established by the order of Poland’s Defense Council on August 11, 1920 to bestow Polish soldiers and officers, who fought against Nazi Germany and showed bravery and valor on the battlefield.
[AWARD_DESC.POL_4]
ORDER ORLA BIALEGO
On February 4, 1921 the Polish Parliament made this Order the highest decoration of Poland for exceptional services to country and people. The Order of the White Eagle had only one class. A person could be bestowed with this order only once.